{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>All of it.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory said (Torat Kohanim here) that this comes to include the horns and the hooves. And it is difficult: why did Abraham not offer the ram as a burnt offering with its horns, etc. (Genesis 22:14), and leave the horns for shofarot, as our Sages of blessed memory stated (Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer 31)? Or perhaps he offered everything and it slipped off the altar, as we learn (Zevaḥim 86a): and all that slipped off the altar — one does not return." "Or it is possible that they were detached from it before the throwing of the blood, and the horns, etc., were not included for the altar-offering except while still attached; but once detached, even if they ascended to the top of the altar they must be taken down, as it is written (Deuteronomy 12:27): ״וְעָשִׂיתָ עֹלֹתֶיךָ הַבָּשָׂר״ etc. — and it is permitted for benefit. And as Rabbi Zeira said regarding the passage 'the altar sanctifies' (Zevaḥim 86a): if it was detached before the throwing of the blood, it is permitted to make from them even a knife-handle." "Or it is possible that the Torah had not yet been given. And although our Sages of blessed memory said (Yoma 28b) that Abraham our father fulfilled even the religious preparation of cooked dishes on the eve of Sabbath, I have already written in the parashot of the patriarchs that this was by way of volition — a voluntary act, for there was no necessity in the matter, for some reason of merit, as you may say the marriage of Tamar to Judah, the marriage of two sisters to Jacob, and likewise the horns of the ram stood as a sign through the instruction of the prophets. And therefore they did not fulfill the future commandment to burn its horns."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:52:32.682648504Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}